  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Uptick in heat and humidity for midweek.\n\nIncreasingly humid air will work its way back into the region\nfor Wednesday as southerly return flow develops along the \nwestern periphery of high pressure. High temperatures will be \naround 2 to 4 degrees warmer than they were today, generally \nranging from the low to mid 80s. Heat index values may touch \nthe 90F mark in the south central mountains on Wednesday as\ndewpoints climb back into the mid to upper 60s. Rain-free \nconditions will prevail for much of the day, though we should\nstart to see thunderstorms develop over northwest PA by the\nevening.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Showers, storms, and gusty winds Wednesday night\ninto Thursday.\n\nBy Wednesday evening, a low pressure system will be moving \nacross northern Ontario with a cold front extending south \nthrough the Great Lakes and Ohio Valley region. The pressure \ngradient will support an uptick in synoptic scale/gradient winds\nWednesday afternoon into Thursday. Expect gusts into the 20s on\nthe hilltops and also in the SE. A gust near 30MPH is possible \non the tops of the Laurels Wed night just before the front. \n\nThe cold front will spark numerous showers and thunderstorms, \nwith the majority occurring before the front passes later\nWednesday night in the north and Thursday morning in the south.\nThere is some potential for severe weather across northwest PA \nwith these storms as confidence in favorable overlap of CAPE and\nshear continues to increase and SPC has added a slight risk of \nsevere weather to highlight this potential. Most guidance shows \nroughly 500 to 1200 J/kg of SBCAPE developing out ahead of the \nfront, with the highest values concentrated over our \nnorthwestern counties. At the same time, an increase in 0-6 km \nbulk shear is expected with values between 40 to 50 knots which\nshould help to organize storms as they develop along the front.\nStorm mode looks to be linear as the deep layer shear should be\nlargely parallel to the front, so damaging wind gusts are \nexpected to be the primary hazard. Model soundings do show some \nturning in the lowest 0-3 km in association with a 40 to 45 knot\nlow-level jet, and a few transient supercell structures or \ncirculations embedded in linear segments cannot be ruled out. \nSPC has included a 2% tornado contour that covers much of the \nslight risk area.\n\nShowers and thunderstorms should weaken as they move south and\neast through the night into a more stable airmass. However, \nthey will still be capable of producing localized heavy \ndownpours. The current WPC outlook for Thurs paints a SLGT risk \nof excessive rainfall over portions of Cambria and Somerset \nCounties. Some CAMs show the potential for up to an inch of rain\nto fall into Thursday morning in the slight risk area, though \nmost guidance would suggest that up to half of an inch of rain \nis the more likely outcome. Even though flash flood guidance is \nrelatively low in these areas, the potential for any flash \nflooding appears to be low at this time unless any areas see \ntraining thunderstorms develop.\n\nHigh pressure builds behind the cold front, giving way to a dry\nand sunny Friday. There could be a few PM SHRA/TSRA Saturday\nand/or Sunday afternoons, especially over the western mtns. A \nmore-active (wetter) pattern is in store for Mon-Tues. But, the\nflat upper ridge over the southern half of the CONUS will make \nit difficult for deep moisture to make it into PA ahead of any \nshots of atmos forcing. The second half of the week probably \nends dry as the upper ridge pumps up over the central CONUS.",
